#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf6a7f40-b2a6-5fd4-9b0f-2e5200133a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295537} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a5b571-7dc4-55fc-95b4-794b2cf751c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295538} {:answer/id #uuid "b73c2a1a-ae93-5d95-a7f2-5a426699abed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295539} {:answer/id #uuid "2269ae32-16f1-57f5-940e-8b5cbf1d092a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295540} {:answer/id #uuid "982f6313-1aff-5013-864c-7f870615840d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b73c2a1a-ae93-5d95-a7f2-5a426699abed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295539} :answer-field/id #uuid "7642a827-a0ad-568b-ba36-f985cbc48b0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1de67dfe-a866-57fb-b3b3-9dacfc2c3c42" :question/math-academy-id "q-103354" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})y & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f414743-3518-5c3d-a8ad-dd3afa4252fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085386} {:answer/id #uuid "aa43c2cc-715b-5f69-be68-a61fc3678251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085387} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ddaf96-aa9e-596c-bcf7-b287cca280b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085388} {:answer/id #uuid "1280bd96-cd25-5c44-9f09-949001006fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085389} {:answer/id #uuid "22b2270d-8c99-5b8d-a182-bfa9b2b5e0ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22b2270d-8c99-5b8d-a182-bfa9b2b5e0ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085390} :answer-field/id #uuid "4563992b-9e65-5236-b672-f6b904701f9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "591724df-60a1-5a9d-9f71-6942e86af15b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331601" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=4$\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=-y$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=4.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $4$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e66b7bb7-de58-5b63-b773-99eeb9f86408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386081} {:answer/id #uuid "db9cfafe-095c-5983-93ef-120b9a0ffabc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386082} {:answer/id #uuid "587cefac-0d76-58d9-bda7-f54c1dcec7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386083} {:answer/id #uuid "ab81c4ed-be1e-5088-ab92-e9a3369ce608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386084} {:answer/id #uuid "418bf5a7-8e73-5736-8336-eac2ebb290b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db9cfafe-095c-5983-93ef-120b9a0ffabc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386082} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cc37fe2-954d-5e08-a1ba-f082d6c3af58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1b74dfd-adf9-57b0-bcdb-a5d1bb8aab44" :question/math-academy-id "q-331743" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=-2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002576cf-2ed9-5b4b-85f5-930244d59605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+3x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295614} {:answer/id #uuid "0d0692bd-bd42-52cf-a2ae-91371e3aa073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295615} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7b52c0-c9b5-553d-82dc-5e67fbc14823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295616} {:answer/id #uuid "6da0902d-c64e-5abc-b9d3-c24e178f8591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295617} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a1ecc5-ec11-54dc-a466-7ca7e0f11cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6da0902d-c64e-5abc-b9d3-c24e178f8591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295617} :answer-field/id #uuid "10b53c59-d11e-5e30-896d-6442479255cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "438bd143-91da-5d1a-8869-3ed754e59004" :question/math-academy-id "q-79754" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-2$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8787ecbb-bcde-5e1b-a5ed-9d23815522e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087109} {:answer/id #uuid "b54535fc-a8dc-5ada-b428-b490dbf1a18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087110} {:answer/id #uuid "c792fe58-82f5-5954-93e9-3119bea36ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{dy}{dx} + x = y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087111} {:answer/id #uuid "6b67e556-e64d-523c-9321-f7fb1846ebc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + x = y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087112} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9de150-76e4-5439-86c8-31f5b6d852a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = \\cos e^{\\arctan (x^{2} + \\ln x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c792fe58-82f5-5954-93e9-3119bea36ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{dy}{dx} + x = y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087111} :answer-field/id #uuid "7759e328-b997-576f-b938-3880d1aeb387" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecc44493-c978-58e2-96eb-083d048c8f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8352" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{-1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $x{y}^{-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}]}
